    {
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10238671",
      "title": "\n            AlphaFold, allosteric, and orthosteric drug discovery: Ways forward - PMC\n        ",
      "publish_date": "2011-03-23",
      "excerpts": [
        "A massive hurdle facing AlphaFold in drug discovery is that proteins exist in ensembles of interconverting conformations, 61 whereas crystal structures and Alphafold’s models provide structural snapshots.\nAlternatively, one can apply molecular dynamics simulations to the AlphaFold model and use the resulting clustered conformations as input to docking methods. This appears to yield mixed performance. Simulations may or may not sample all relevant states.\nDel Alamo D, Sala D, McHaourab HS, Meiler J. Sampling alternative conformational states of transporters and receptors with AlphaFold2. Elife. 2022;11:e75751. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]\n129. Ma P, Li DW, Brüschweiler R. Predicting protein flexibility with AlphaFold. Proteins. 2023. doi: 10.1002/prot.26471."
      ]
    },
    {
      "url": "https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10569795",
      "title": "High-throughput prediction of protein conformational distributions with subsampled AlphaFold2 | NSF Public Access Repository",
      "publish_date": "2024-12-01",
      "excerpts": [
        "While AlphaFold 2 has shown exceptional accuracy and speed, it is designed to predict proteins’ ground state conformations and is limited in its ability to predict conformational landscapes.\nAbstract Deep learning approaches like AlphaFold 2 (AF2) have revolutionized structural biology by accurately predicting the ground state structures of proteins.\nRecently, clustering and subsampling techniques that manipulate multiple sequence alignment (MSA) inputs into AlphaFold to generate conformational ensembles of proteins have also been proposed.\nUniquely, by leveraging the subsampling of MSAs, our tool enables the generation of alternative protein conformations.\nWe have found that AF2 generates both a cluster of open and a cluster of closed models for proteins that have comparable numbers of open and closed structures in the PDB and not too many other conformations. This was observed even with default MSA parameters, thus without further subsampling.\nIn contrast, with the exception of a single protein, AF2 did not yield multiple clusters of conformations for proteins that had imbalanced numbers of open and closed structures in the PDB, or had substantial numbers of other structures. Subsampling improved the results only for a single protein, but very shallow MSA led to incorrect structures.\nStarting from ground state static structures, this integrative method generates alternative structures as well as pathways of protein conformational motions, using a progressive enhancement of the energetic frustration features in the input multiple sequence alignment sequences.\nThe physical model is sensitive to sequence-dependent properties and conformational changes required for binding, while information accelerates sampling of bound conformations.\nDynamicBind accurately recovers ligand-specific conformations from unbound protein structures without the need for holo-structures or extensive sampling. Remarkably, it demonstrates state-of-the-art performance in docking and virtual screening benchmarks.\nWhile AlphaFold 2 has shown exceptional accuracy and speed, it is designed to predict proteins’ ground state conformations and is limited in its ability to predict conformational landscapes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-66870-x",
      "title": "Rapid estimation of protein folding pathways from sequence alone using AlphaFold2 | Nature Communications",
      "publish_date": "2025-12-01",
      "excerpts": [
        "For instance, modifying multiple sequence alignments (MSAs) leverages different co-evolutionary signals in AF2 to identify multiple biologically relevant states <sup>[5](#ref-CR5 \"Alamo, D. D., Sala, D., Mchaourab, H. S. & Meiler, J. Sampling alternative conformational states of transporters and receptors with AlphaFold2.\n13 , 176 (2022).\nArticle ADS PubMed PubMed Central Google Scholar\n4. Chang, L. & Perez, A. Ranking Peptide Binders by Affinity with AlphaFold . Angewandte Chemie International Edition (2022).\n5. Alamo, D. D., Sala, D., Mchaourab, H. S. & Meiler, J. Sampling alternative conformational states of transporters and receptors with AlphaFold2."
      ]
